    "sourceWikitext": "{{Recipe summary\n| Category = Rice recipes\n| Difficulty = 2\n}}\n\n{{recipe}}\n\n==Ingredients==\n*1 large (~2 [[Cookbook:Pound|lbs]] / 1 [[Cookbook:Kilogram|kg]]) whole [[Cookbook:Fish|fish]], cleaned \n*1 [[Cookbook:Cup|cup]] [[Cookbook:Olive Oil|olive oil]]\n*2 cups uncooked [[Cookbook:Rice|rice]] \n*1 ½ cups [[Cookbook:Chopping|chopped]] [[Cookbook:Onion|onion]] \n*[[Cookbook:Salt|Salt]]\n*[[Cookbook:Lemon Juice|Lemon juice]] \n*Water\n\n== Procedure ==\n# [[Cookbook:Frying|Fry]] fish in olive oil until well cooked. Remove the fish and allow to cool.\n# Fry the chopped onions in the same oil until well browned. Meanwhile, debone the fried fish, reserving the cooked fillets to be served over the rice.\n# Add the head, bones and skin of the fish to the fried onions and cook with 4 cups of water in a [[Cookbook:Pressure Cooking|pressure cooker]] for 15 minutes, or for a longer time in an open pan. \n# [[Cookbook:Straining|Strain]] the broth and [[Cookbook:Boiling|boil]] the rice in it using 1–4 cups of broth for each cup of rice (this will depend on the absorbency of the rice you use).\n# [[Cookbook:Simmering|Simmer]] the rice on low heat so that most of the broth is absorbed. \n\n== Notes, tips, and variations ==\n\n* Some of the broth may be flavored with a few drops of lemon and served on the side as a sauce for this dish.\n\n[[Category:Recipes using rice]]\n[[Category:Recipes using fish]]\n[[Category:Lebanese recipes]]\n[[Category:Pan fried recipes]]\n[[Category:Boiled recipes]]\n[[Category:Main course recipes]]\n[[Category:Recipes using lemon juice]]",
    "isRecipe": true,
